            <ArticleTitle>Dual origin of the left coronary artery with vestigial branch arising from the aorta and main trunk arising from the pulmonary artery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>373-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 3-month-old infant presented with cardiomegaly, mitral insufficiency, myocardial ischemia, and congestive heart failure suggestive of anomalous left coronary artery. A vestigial left coronary artery was demonstrated on angiography and was thought to represent the only abnormality. At autopsy, the infant had a vestigial left coronary artery arising from the aorta but in addition had a normal caliber anomalous left coronary artery arising from the pulmonary artery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
